---
title: Server islands
summary: HTML fetched from the server after the page loads. Placeholder first, then real HTML — no JS required.
related:
  - islands/timing-nesting
  - app/streaming-ppr
  - data-state/partials
---

A **server island** is a hole in the page whose HTML is fetched from the server *after* the initial load. The page ships a placeholder; the real HTML swaps in on a schedule. No JS hydrates it — it is server HTML, just delivered late.

> **When to use.** A server island is the answer when a piece of the page is **request-specific but not interactive** — a personalized greeting, a "you have 3 unread" count, a slow upstream you don't want to block first paint. It needs no client JS, so it is cheaper than a client island. If it needs interactivity too, add `hydrate` (see below). If it can be prerendered, it does not need to be deferred at all.

Mark the import with `defer` instead of `hydrate`:

```svelte
<script>
  import Greeting from './Greeting.svelte' with { fill: 'load' };
</script>

<Greeting name="Ada" />
```

`Greeting` renders on the server, signed per request, and its HTML fills the hole. This is the right tool when a partial depends on the request (cookies, headers, a slow upstream) but needs no interactivity.

The demo below is a real server island on this page. Its timestamp is stamped when the island's HTML is fetched — *after* the page loaded — not at page render, which is how you can tell it came from the server later.

<script>
  import ServerGreeting from './ServerGreeting.svelte' with { fill: 'load' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo">
  <ServerGreeting salutation="Aloha">
    {#snippet ogygiaFallback()}
      <p class="doc-demo-fallback">Loading from the server…</p>
    {/snippet}
  </ServerGreeting>
</div>

## The same schedules

`defer` uses the same timing words as `hydrate` (minus `interaction`, which is hydrate-only) — here they mean *when the placeholder is replaced with real HTML*:

| Value | HTML arrives |
| --- | --- |
| `load` | right away |
| `idle` | when the browser is idle |
| `visible` | when scrolled into view |
| `'(max-width: 600px)'` | when the media query matches |

## A fallback while it loads

Pass an `ogygiaFallback` snippet — it shows in the placeholder until the real HTML lands:

```svelte
<Greeting name="Ada">
  {#snippet ogygiaFallback()}
    <p class="skeleton">Loading…</p>
  {/snippet}
</Greeting>
```

## Deferred client islands

Combine both axes and you get HTML later, then JS on that DOM:

```svelte
<script>
  import Cart from './Cart.svelte' with { fill: 'visible', wake: 'load' };
</script>
```

`defer` is phase one (fetch + swap); `hydrate` is phase two (import + hydrate). Matching schedules coalesce: after the swap, `wake: 'load'` runs immediately with no second wait.

> `wake: 'none'` with any `defer` is nonsense (HTML later and no JS) — use `defer` alone. Dev warns and treats it as defer-only.

Server islands need the [server handle](/docs/start/install) installed, since they load through the signed island endpoint.

## Do / don't

- **Do** give a server island an `ogygiaFallback` so the placeholder is not an empty gap while its HTML loads.
- **Do** reach for `fill: 'load'` with `ogygia({ stream: true })` when a page has several holes — they stream down one response instead of a fetch each. See [Streaming & PPR](/docs/app/streaming-ppr).
- **Don't** use a server island for interactive UI — that is a [client island](/docs/islands/client-islands). Combine both axes only when you genuinely need HTML-later *and* interactivity.
- **Don't** pair `wake: 'none'` with `defer` — it is defer-only; dev warns.


